#{[17592186538237 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186538285 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186538313 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}28 & 18 \\\\ 44 & 24\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186538328 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186538339 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186538366 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}28 & 44 \\\\ 18 & 24\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186538368 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}18 \\\\ 24\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186538397 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186538408 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186538437 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 18 \\\\ 44 & 24\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186538497 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186538529 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186538608 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -2 \\\\ 13 & -5\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186538629 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ -13 & 5\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186538743 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -5 & 3\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186538747 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186538757 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ 3x \\\\ -5x \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186538762 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}18 & 24\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186538800 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186538806 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186538934 :question/problem "Consider a function $f:{R}^{3\\times 3}\\to {R}^{3}$ defined by\n\n$$\nf(A)=A\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nGiven that ${a}_{ij}$ denotes the entry of $A$ in the intersection of the $i$th row and $j$th column, we have\n\n$\\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂{a}_{31}}=${{field-1}}."] [17592186538934 :question/worked-solution "We can write the function $f$ as\n\n$$\nf(A)=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} & {a}_{13} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22} & {a}_{23} \\\\ {a}_{31} & {a}_{32} & {a}_{33}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2{a}_{11}+3{a}_{12}-{a}_{13} \\\\ 2{a}_{21}+3{a}_{22}-{a}_{23} \\\\ 2{a}_{31}+3{a}_{32}-{a}_{33}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂{a}_{31}}=\\frac{∂}{∂{a}_{31}}(2{a}_{31}+3{a}_{32}-{a}_{33})=2.\n$$"] [17592186538947 :question/problem "Consider the functions $y(x)=Ax$ and $f(y)=By,$ where $x\\in {R}^{2},y\\in {R}^{3},$ and the matrices $A$ and $B$ are given by\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat is $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))$ evaluated at ${x}_{0}=[0,\\,1]^{T}?$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"2\" width=\"30px\">\n${\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))|}_{{x}_{0}}=\\,\\,$\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$11$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [17592186538947 :question/worked-solution "Using the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each of the factors in turn.\n\n- The function $f$ acts on a vector $y\\in {R}^{3}$ by multiplying it by $B$ and outputs a vector $f(y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}=B.\n$$\n\n- The function $y$ acts on a vector $x\\in {R}^{2}$ by multiplying it by $A$ and outputs a vector $y(x)\\in {R}^{3}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=A.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x)) & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =BA \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 15 \\\\ 11 & 15\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186538960 :question/problem "Consider the functions $y:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{2}$ and $f:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2},$ defined by\n\n$$\ny(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{1}+{x}_{2}{x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,f(y)=\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1}-{y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat is \n$\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))$ evaluated at \n$$\n\\displaystyle {x}_{0}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"2\" width=\"30px\">\n${\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))|}_{{x}_{0}}=\\,\\,$\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$38$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$34$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [17592186538960 :question/worked-solution "Using the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each of the factors in turn.\n\n- The function $f$ acts on a vector $y\\in {R}^{2}$ and outputs a vector $f(y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus, the total derivative of $f$ with respect to $y$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{y}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{y}_{2}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{1}}({y}_{1}-{y}_{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{2}}({y}_{1}-{y}_{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{1}}({y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{2}}({y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2})\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2{y}_{1} & 2{y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $$\n\\displaystyle {y}_{0}=y({x}_{0})=\\begin{bmatrix}3\\cdot 2+1 \\\\ 3+2\\cdot 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 14 & 10\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- The function $y$ acts on a vector $x\\in {R}^{3}$ and outputs a vector $y(x)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus, the total derivative of $y$ with respect to $x$ is a $2\\times 3$ matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{y}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{y}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & \\frac{∂{y}_{1}}{∂{x}_{3}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{y}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{y}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & \\frac{∂{y}_{2}}{∂{x}_{3}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}({x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}({x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{3}}({x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}{x}_{3}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}{x}_{3}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{3}}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}{x}_{3})\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{2} & {x}_{1} & 1 \\\\ 1 & {x}_{3} & {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $$\n\\displaystyle {x}_{0}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{{x}_{0}}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x)){|}_{{x}_{0}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 14 & 10\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 38 & 52 & 34\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186538967 :question/problem "Consider the vector-valued function $f:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{3}$ defined by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nWhat is $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x},$ the total derivative of $f$ with respect to $x$ at $$\n\\displaystyle {x}_{0}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$"] [17592186538967 :question/worked-solution "The total derivative of a vector-valued function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{m}$ with respect to a vector $x\\in {R}^{n}$ is the $m\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}\\in {R}^{m\\times n}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, writing out the function $f(x)$ where $$\n\\displaystyle x=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\in {R}^{3},\n$$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{1}+{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2}+{x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, the total derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{3}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{3}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂{x}_{2}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂{x}_{3}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{3}}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}({x}_{1}+{x}_{3}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}({x}_{1}+{x}_{3}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{3}}({x}_{1}+{x}_{3}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}({x}_{2}+{x}_{3}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}({x}_{2}+{x}_{3}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{3}}({x}_{2}+{x}_{3})\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the total derivative is a constant matrix, evaluating it at $$\n\\displaystyle {x}_{0}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ gives the same matrix."] [17592186538983 :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are eigenvectors that correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=3$ of the matrix $A,$ given above. Find the third eigenvector ${v}_{3}=[a,\\,b,\\,c]^{T}$ such that the set $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent. What is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$"] [17592186538983 :question/worked-solution "First, we find all the eigenvalues of $A$ by solving the characteristic equation $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}1-\\lambda  & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda  & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )\\begin{vmatrix}1-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )((1-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-(2)(2)) & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -3) & =0 \\\\ -(\\lambda -2)(\\lambda +1)(\\lambda -3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=3,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-1.$ Since we obtained three distinct eigenvalues, the set of eigenvectors $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\},$ where each vector corresponds to different eigenvalue, will be linearly independent.\n\nNow, recall that ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=3.$ Thus, the third eigenvector ${v}_{3}$ must correspond to ${\\lambda }_{3}=-1.$ \n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =-1.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}-(-1)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}-4{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}2 & 2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 0\\end{array}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}2 & 2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 0\\end{array}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+\\frac{3}{8}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}2 & 2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $3$rd). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{3}=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}=-{x}_{2}.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the following eigenvector:\n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{-1}{1}=-1.$"] [17592186539024 :question/problem "Consider the function $f:R\\to {R}^{4},$ defined by \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the total derivative of $f$ with respect to $x?$"] [17592186539024 :question/worked-solution "The total derivative of a vector-valued function $f:R\\to {R}^{m}$ with respect to a scalar $x$ is the column vector\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂x}\\end{bmatrix}\\in {R}^{m\\times 1}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the function \n\n- acts on a scalar $x\\in R,$ and\n\n- outputs a vector $f(x)\\in {R}^{4}$ defined by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}x+2 \\\\ 3x \\\\ 4x-10 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂x} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{4}}{∂x}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+2) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}(3x) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}(4x-10) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}(6)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\in {R}^{4\\times 1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186539029 :question/problem "Consider the functions $y:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ and $f:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{3},$ defined by\n\n$$\ny(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}^{3} \\\\ -{x}_{1}{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,f(y)=\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1}-{y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{2} \\\\ 2{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}^{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat is \n$\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))$ evaluated at \n$$\n\\displaystyle {x}_{0}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"30px\">\n${\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))|}_{{x}_{0}}=\\,\\,$\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">$-3$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">$2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [17592186539029 :question/worked-solution "Using the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each of the factors in turn.\n\n- The function $f$ acts on a vector $y\\in {R}^{2}$ and outputs a vector $f(y)\\in {R}^{3}.$ Thus, the total derivative of $f$ with respect to $y$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{y}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{y}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂{y}_{2}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{1}}({y}_{1}-{y}_{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{2}}({y}_{1}-{y}_{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{1}}({y}_{2}^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{2}}({y}_{2}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{1}}(2{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}^{3}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{2}}(2{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}^{3})\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2{y}_{2} \\\\ 2 & 3{y}_{2}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $$\n\\displaystyle {y}_{0}=y({x}_{0})=\\begin{bmatrix}0-{1}^{3} \\\\ -1\\cdot 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- The function $y$ acts on a vector $x\\in {R}^{2}$ and outputs a vector $y(x)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus, the total derivative of $y$ with respect to $x$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{y}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{y}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{y}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{y}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}({x}_{2}-{x}_{1}^{3}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}({x}_{2}-{x}_{1}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}(-{x}_{1}{x}_{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}(-{x}_{1}{x}_{2})\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3{x}_{1}^{2} & 1 \\\\ -{x}_{2} & -{x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $$\n\\displaystyle {x}_{0}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{{x}_{0}}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x)){|}_{{x}_{0}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186539293 :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D,$\n\n$$\n1\\le x\\le 5,\\quad 2\\le x^{2} + 4 y\\le 3,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x\\,\\text{and}\\,v=x^{2} + 4 y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v):1\\le u\\le 5,\\,2\\le v\\le 3\\}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x}&\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y}\\\\\\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x}&\\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y}\\end{vmatrix}\\\\&=\\left|\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0\\\\2 x & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\right|\\\\&=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)}\\right)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\ne0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\nSolving the transformation equations gives $x=u,\\ y=- \\frac{u^{2}}{4} + \\frac{v}{4}$, so the transformation is one-to-one on this domain.\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D e^{x^{2} + x + 4 y}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y & =\\iint_D e^{x+(x^{2} + 4 y)}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y \\\\ & =\\iint_\\Delta e^{u + v}\\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =\\iint_\\Delta \\left(e^{u + v}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int_{1}^{5} [\\int_{2}^{3} e^{u} e^{v}\\,\\mathrm{d}v]\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int_{1}^{5} \\left(e^{u}\\right)[\\int_{2}^{3} e^{v}\\,\\mathrm{d}v]\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int_{1}^{5} \\left(e^{u}\\right)\\left[e^{v}\\right]_{2}^{3}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int_{1}^{5} \\left(e^{u}\\right)\\left(\\left(e^{3}\\right)-(\\left(e^{2}\\right))\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ & =\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)\\cdot \\left(- e^{2} + e^{3}\\right)\\int_{1}^{5} e^{u}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ & =\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)\\cdot \\left(- e^{2} + e^{3}\\right)\\left[e^{u}\\right]_{1}^{5} \\\\ & =\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)\\cdot \\left(- e^{2} + e^{3}\\right)\\left(\\left(e^{5}\\right)-(\\left(e\\right))\\right) \\\\ & =\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)\\cdot \\left(e^{5} - e\\right)\\cdot \\left(- e^{2} + e^{3}\\right) \\\\ & =\\frac{e^{3}}{4} - \\frac{e^{4}}{4} - \\frac{e^{7}}{4} + \\frac{e^{8}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186539294 :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D,$\n\n$$\n1\\le x\\le 3,\\quad 2\\le x + 3 y\\le 3,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x\\,\\text{and}\\,v=x + 3 y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v):1\\le u\\le 3,\\,2\\le v\\le 3\\}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x}&\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y}\\\\\\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x}&\\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y}\\end{vmatrix}\\\\&=\\left|\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0\\\\1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\right|\\\\&=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)}\\right)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{3}=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\ne0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\nSolving the transformation equations gives $x=u,\\ y=- \\frac{u}{3} + \\frac{v}{3}$, so the transformation is one-to-one on this domain.\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D e^{2 x + 6 y} \\left(x - 1\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y & =\\iint_\\Delta e^{2 v} \\left(u - 1\\right)\\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =\\iint_\\Delta \\left(e^{2 v} \\left(u - 1\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int_{1}^{3} [\\int_{2}^{3} e^{2 v} \\left(u - 1\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v]\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int_{1}^{3} \\left(u - 1\\right)[\\int_{2}^{3} e^{2 v}\\,\\mathrm{d}v]\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int_{1}^{3} \\left(u - 1\\right)\\left[\\frac{e^{2 v}}{2}\\right]_{2}^{3}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int_{1}^{3} \\left(u - 1\\right)\\left(\\left(\\frac{e^{6}}{2}\\right)-(\\left(\\frac{e^{4}}{2}\\right))\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ & =\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\cdot \\left(- \\frac{e^{4}}{2} + \\frac{e^{6}}{2}\\right)\\int_{1}^{3} u - 1\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ & =\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\cdot \\left(- \\frac{e^{4}}{2} + \\frac{e^{6}}{2}\\right)\\left[\\frac{u^{2}}{2} - u\\right]_{1}^{3} \\\\ & =\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\cdot \\left(- \\frac{e^{4}}{2} + \\frac{e^{6}}{2}\\right)\\left(\\left(\\frac{9}{2}\\right)+\\left(-3\\right)-(\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)+\\left(-1\\right))\\right) \\\\ & =\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\cdot \\left(2\\right)\\cdot \\left(- \\frac{e^{4}}{2} + \\frac{e^{6}}{2}\\right) \\\\ & =- \\frac{e^{4}}{3} + \\frac{e^{6}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186539297 :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D,$\n\n$$\n2\\le x\\le 3,\\quad 2\\le x + 3 y\\le 3,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x\\,\\text{and}\\,v=x + 3 y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v):2\\le u\\le 3,\\,2\\le v\\le 3\\}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x}&\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y}\\\\\\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x}&\\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y}\\end{vmatrix}\\\\&=\\left|\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0\\\\1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\right|\\\\&=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)}\\right)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{3}=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\ne0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\nSolving the transformation equations gives $x=u,\\ y=- \\frac{u}{3} + \\frac{v}{3}$, so the transformation is one-to-one on this domain.\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D e^{2 x + 6 y} \\left(x - 1\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y & =\\iint_\\Delta e^{2 v} \\left(u - 1\\right)\\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =\\iint_\\Delta \\left(e^{2 v} \\left(u - 1\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int_{2}^{3} [\\int_{2}^{3} e^{2 v} \\left(u - 1\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v]\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int_{2}^{3} \\left(u - 1\\right)[\\int_{2}^{3} e^{2 v}\\,\\mathrm{d}v]\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int_{2}^{3} \\left(u - 1\\right)\\left[\\frac{e^{2 v}}{2}\\right]_{2}^{3}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int_{2}^{3} \\left(u - 1\\right)\\left(\\left(\\frac{e^{6}}{2}\\right)-(\\left(\\frac{e^{4}}{2}\\right))\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ & =\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\cdot \\left(- \\frac{e^{4}}{2} + \\frac{e^{6}}{2}\\right)\\int_{2}^{3} u - 1\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ & =\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\cdot \\left(- \\frac{e^{4}}{2} + \\frac{e^{6}}{2}\\right)\\left[\\frac{u^{2}}{2} - u\\right]_{2}^{3} \\\\ & =\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\cdot \\left(- \\frac{e^{4}}{2} + \\frac{e^{6}}{2}\\right)\\left(\\left(\\frac{9}{2}\\right)+\\left(-3\\right)-(\\left(2\\right)+\\left(-2\\right))\\right) \\\\ & =\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)\\cdot \\left(- \\frac{e^{4}}{2} + \\frac{e^{6}}{2}\\right) \\\\ & =- \\frac{e^{4}}{4} + \\frac{e^{6}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186539305 :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D,$\n\n$$\n0\\le x\\le 7,\\quad 0\\le x + y\\le 3,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x\\,\\text{and}\\,v=x + y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v):0\\le u\\le 7,\\,0\\le v\\le 3\\}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x}&\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y}\\\\\\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x}&\\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y}\\end{vmatrix}\\\\&=\\left|\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0\\\\1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\right|\\\\&=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)}\\right)^{-1}=1=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\ne0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\nSolving the transformation equations gives $x=u,\\ y=- u + v$, so the transformation is one-to-one on this domain.\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D x + y\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y & =\\iint_\\Delta v\\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =\\iint_\\Delta \\left(v\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =1\\int_{0}^{3} [\\int_{0}^{7} v\\,\\mathrm{d}u]\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =1\\int_{0}^{3} v[\\int_{0}^{7} 1\\,\\mathrm{d}u]\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =1\\int_{0}^{3} v\\left[u\\right]_{0}^{7}\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =1\\int_{0}^{3} v\\left(\\left(7\\right)-(\\left(0\\right))\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(7\\right)\\int_{0}^{3} v\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(7\\right)\\left[\\frac{v^{2}}{2}\\right]_{0}^{3} \\\\ & =\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(7\\right)\\left(\\left(\\frac{9}{2}\\right)-(\\left(0\\right))\\right) \\\\ & =\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(7\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{9}{2}\\right) \\\\ & =\\frac{63}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186539318 :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, 