 domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v):0\\le u\\le 2,\\,0\\le v\\le 6\\}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x}&\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y}\\\\\\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x}&\\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y}\\end{vmatrix}\\\\&=\\left|\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0\\\\1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\right|\\\\&=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)}\\right)^{-1}=1=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\ne0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\nSolving the transformation equations gives $x=u,\\ y=- u + v$, so the transformation is one-to-one on this domain.\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D x + y\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y & =\\iint_\\Delta v\\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =\\iint_\\Delta \\left(v\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =1\\int_{0}^{6} [\\int_{0}^{2} v\\,\\mathrm{d}u]\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =1\\int_{0}^{6} v[\\int_{0}^{2} 1\\,\\mathrm{d}u]\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =1\\int_{0}^{6} v\\left[u\\right]_{0}^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =1\\int_{0}^{6} v\\left(\\left(2\\right)-(\\left(0\\right))\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(2\\right)\\int_{0}^{6} v\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(2\\right)\\left[\\frac{v^{2}}{2}\\right]_{0}^{6} \\\\ & =\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(2\\right)\\left(\\left(18\\right)-(\\left(0\\right))\\right) \\\\ & =\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(2\\right)\\cdot \\left(18\\right) \\\\ & =36\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186539333 :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D,$\n\n$$\n0\\le x\\le 4,\\quad 0\\le x + y\\le 2,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x\\,\\text{and}\\,v=x + y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v):0\\le u\\le 4,\\,0\\le v\\le 2\\}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x}&\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y}\\\\\\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x}&\\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y}\\end{vmatrix}\\\\&=\\left|\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0\\\\1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\right|\\\\&=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)}\\right)^{-1}=1=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\ne0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\nSolving the transformation equations gives $x=u,\\ y=- u + v$, so the transformation is one-to-one on this domain.\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D x + y\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y & =\\iint_\\Delta v\\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =\\iint_\\Delta \\left(v\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =1\\int_{0}^{2} [\\int_{0}^{4} v\\,\\mathrm{d}u]\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =1\\int_{0}^{2} v[\\int_{0}^{4} 1\\,\\mathrm{d}u]\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =1\\int_{0}^{2} v\\left[u\\right]_{0}^{4}\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =1\\int_{0}^{2} v\\left(\\left(4\\right)-(\\left(0\\right))\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(4\\right)\\int_{0}^{2} v\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(4\\right)\\left[\\frac{v^{2}}{2}\\right]_{0}^{2} \\\\ & =\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(4\\right)\\left(\\left(2\\right)-(\\left(0\\right))\\right) \\\\ & =\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(4\\right)\\cdot \\left(2\\right) \\\\ & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186539336 :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D,$\n\n$$\n2\\le \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{16 - y^{2}}}\\le 5,\\quad 2\\le y\\le 3,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{16 - y^{2}}}\\,\\text{and}\\,v=y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v):2\\le u\\le 5,\\,2\\le v\\le 3\\}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x}&\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y}\\\\\\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x}&\\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y}\\end{vmatrix}\\\\&=\\left|\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{16 - y^{2}}} & \\frac{x y}{\\left(16 - y^{2}\\right)^{\\frac{3}{2}}}\\\\0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\right|\\\\&=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{16 - y^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)}\\right)^{-1}=\\sqrt{16 - y^{2}}=\\sqrt{16 - v^{2}}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\ne0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\nSolving the transformation equations gives $x=u \\sqrt{16 - v^{2}},\\ y=v$, so the transformation is one-to-one on this domain.\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D x y^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y & =\\iint_\\Delta u v^{2} \\sqrt{16 - v^{2}}\\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =\\iint_\\Delta \\left(u v^{2} \\sqrt{16 - v^{2}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\sqrt{16 - v^{2}}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =\\iint_\\Delta u v^2(16-v^2)\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =\\int_{2}^{5} \\left(u\\right)[\\int_{2}^{3} - v^{4} + 16 v^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}v]\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ & =1\\int_{2}^{5} \\left(u\\right)\\left[- \\frac{v^{5}}{5} + \\frac{16 v^{3}}{3}\\right]_{2}^{3}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ & =1\\int_{2}^{5} \\left(u\\right)\\left(\\left(- \\frac{243}{5}\\right)+\\left(144\\right)-(\\left(- \\frac{32}{5}\\right)+\\left(\\frac{128}{3}\\right))\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ & =\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{887}{15}\\right)\\int_{2}^{5} u\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ & =\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{887}{15}\\right)\\left[\\frac{u^{2}}{2}\\right]_{2}^{5} \\\\ & =\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{887}{15}\\right)\\left(\\left(\\frac{25}{2}\\right)-(\\left(2\\right))\\right) \\\\ & =\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{21}{2}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{887}{15}\\right) \\\\ & =\\frac{6209}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186539352 :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D,$\n\n$$\n0\\le x\\le 7,\\quad 0\\le x + y\\le 5,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x\\,\\text{and}\\,v=x + y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v):0\\le u\\le 7,\\,0\\le v\\le 5\\}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x}&\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y}\\\\\\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x}&\\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y}\\end{vmatrix}\\\\&=\\left|\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0\\\\1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\right|\\\\&=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)}\\right)^{-1}=1=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\ne0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\nSolving the transformation equations gives $x=u,\\ y=- u + v$, so the transformation is one-to-one on this domain.\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D x + y\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y & =\\iint_\\Delta v\\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =\\iint_\\Delta \\left(v\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =1\\int_{0}^{5} [\\int_{0}^{7} v\\,\\mathrm{d}u]\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =1\\int_{0}^{5} v[\\int_{0}^{7} 1\\,\\mathrm{d}u]\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =1\\int_{0}^{5} v\\left[u\\right]_{0}^{7}\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =1\\int_{0}^{5} v\\left(\\left(7\\right)-(\\left(0\\right))\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(7\\right)\\int_{0}^{5} v\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(7\\right)\\left[\\frac{v^{2}}{2}\\right]_{0}^{5} \\\\ & =\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(7\\right)\\left(\\left(\\frac{25}{2}\\right)-(\\left(0\\right))\\right) \\\\ & =\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(7\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{25}{2}\\right) \\\\ & =\\frac{175}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186539354 :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D,$\n\n$$\n1\\le x\\le 3,\\quad 1\\le x^{2} + 3 y\\le 3,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x\\,\\text{and}\\,v=x^{2} + 3 y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v):1\\le u\\le 3,\\,1\\le v\\le 3\\}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x}&\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y}\\\\\\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x}&\\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y}\\end{vmatrix}\\\\&=\\left|\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0\\\\2 x & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\right|\\\\&=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)}\\right)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{3}=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\ne0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\nSolving the transformation equations gives $x=u,\\ y=- \\frac{u^{2}}{3} + \\frac{v}{3}$, so the transformation is one-to-one on this domain.\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D e^{x^{2} + x + 3 y}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y & =\\iint_D e^{x+(x^{2} + 3 y)}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y \\\\ & =\\iint_\\Delta e^{u + v}\\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =\\iint_\\Delta \\left(e^{u + v}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int_{1}^{3} [\\int_{1}^{3} e^{u} e^{v}\\,\\mathrm{d}v]\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int_{1}^{3} \\left(e^{u}\\right)[\\int_{1}^{3} e^{v}\\,\\mathrm{d}v]\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int_{1}^{3} \\left(e^{u}\\right)\\left[e^{v}\\right]_{1}^{3}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int_{1}^{3} \\left(e^{u}\\right)\\left(\\left(e^{3}\\right)-(\\left(e\\right))\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ & =\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\cdot \\left(e^{3} - e\\right)\\int_{1}^{3} e^{u}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ & =\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\cdot \\left(e^{3} - e\\right)\\left[e^{u}\\right]_{1}^{3} \\\\ & =\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\cdot \\left(e^{3} - e\\right)\\left(\\left(e^{3}\\right)-(\\left(e\\right))\\right) \\\\ & =\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\cdot \\left(e^{3} - e\\right)\\cdot \\left(e^{3} - e\\right) \\\\ & =\\frac{e^{2}}{3} - \\frac{2 e^{4}}{3} + \\frac{e^{6}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186539366 :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|du\\,dv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}},\\,v=xy\n$$\n\n \ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 4,\\,4\\le v\\le 5\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-\\frac{2y}{{x}^{3}} & \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ y & \\text{ }\\text{ }x\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3y}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{\\left(-\\frac{3y}{{x}^{2}}\\right)}=-\\frac{1}{3u}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(xy)^{2}\\,dx\\,dy & ={∬}_{Δ}{v}^{2}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{v}^{2}\\cdot |\\frac{1}{3u}|\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{4}^{5}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{{v}^{2}}{u}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{4}^{5}{v}^{2}[{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u]\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{4}^{5}{v}^{2}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|\\,{|}_{1}^{4}]dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{4}^{5}{v}^{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1)\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{3}{\\int }_{4}^{5}{v}^{2}\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}{v}^{3}\\,{|}_{4}^{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{9}\\cdot (125-64) \\\\  & =\\frac{122\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186539371 :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|du\\,dv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}},\\,v=xy\n$$\n\n \ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 16,\\,2\\le v\\le 5\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-\\frac{2y}{{x}^{3}} & \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ y & \\text{ }\\text{ }x\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3y}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{\\left(-\\frac{3y}{{x}^{2}}\\right)}=-\\frac{1}{3u}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(xy)^{2}\\,dx\\,dy & ={∬}_{Δ}{v}^{2}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{v}^{2}\\cdot |\\frac{1}{3u}|\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{2}^{5}{\\int }_{1}^{16}\\frac{{v}^{2}}{u}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{2}^{5}{v}^{2}[{\\int }_{1}^{16}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u]\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{2}^{5}{v}^{2}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|\\,{|}_{1}^{16}]dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{2}^{5}{v}^{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡16-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1)\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{3}{\\int }_{2}^{5}{v}^{2}\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}{v}^{3}\\,{|}_{2}^{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{9}\\cdot (125-8) \\\\  & =26\\operatorname{ln}⁡4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186539379 :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|du\\,dv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}},\\,v=xy\n$$\n\n \ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 9,\\,2\\le v\\le 4\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-\\frac{2y}{{x}^{3}} & \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ y & \\text{ }\\text{ }x\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3y}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{\\left(-\\frac{3y}{{x}^{2}}\\right)}=-\\frac{1}{3u}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(xy)^{2}\\,dx\\,dy & ={∬}_{Δ}{v}^{2}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{v}^{2}\\cdot |\\frac{1}{3u}|\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{2}^{4}{\\int }_{1}^{9}\\frac{{v}^{2}}{u}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{2}^{4}{v}^{2}[{\\int }_{1}^{9}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u]\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{2}^{4}{v}^{2}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|\\,{|}_{1}^{9}]dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{2}^{4}{v}^{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡9-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1)\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{3}{\\int }_{2}^{4}{v}^{2}\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}{v}^{3}\\,{|}_{2}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{9}\\cdot (64-8) \\\\  & =\\frac{112\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186539380 :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|du\\,dv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}},\\,v=xy\n$$\n\n \ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 16,\\,1\\le v\\le 4\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-\\frac{2y}{{x}^{3}} & \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ y & \\text{ }\\text{ }x\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3y}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{\\left(-\\frac{3y}{{x}^{2}}\\right)}=-\\frac{1}{3u}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(xy)^{2}\\,dx\\,dy & ={∬}_{Δ}{v}^{2}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{v}^{2}\\cdot |\\frac{1}{3u}|\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{1}^{16}\\frac{{v}^{2}}{u}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{v}^{2}[{\\int }_{1}^{16}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u]\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{v}^{2}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|\\,{|}_{1}^{16}]dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{v}^{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡16-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1)\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{v}^{2}\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}{v}^{3}\\,{|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{9}\\cdot (64-1) \\\\  & =14\\operatorname{ln}⁡4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186539387 :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|du\\,dv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}},\\,v=xy\n$$\n\n \ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 4,\\,5\\le v\\le 6\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-\\frac{2y}{{x}^{3}} & \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ y & \\text{ }\\text{ }x\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3y}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{\\left(-\\frac{3y}{{x}^{2}}\\right)}=-\\frac{1}{3u}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(xy)^{2}\\,dx\\,dy & ={∬}_{Δ}{v}^{2}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{v}^{2}\\cdot |\\frac{1}{3u}|\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{5}^{6}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{{v}^{2}}{u}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{5}^{6}{v}^{2}[{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u]\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{5}^{6}{v}^{2}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|\\,{|}_{1}^{4}]dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{5}^{6}{v}^{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1)\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{3}{\\int }_{5}^{6}{v}^{2}\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}{v}^{3}\\,{|}_{5}^{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{9}\\cdot (216-125) \\\\  & =\\frac{182\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186539481 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 22\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186539510 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186539539 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186539553 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}30 \\\\ 42\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186539639 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}30 & 42\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186539679 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186539700 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}21 \\\\ 15\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186539703 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & -29\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186539752 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}58 & 26\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186539756 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186539776 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}58 \\\\ 26\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186539828 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-7 \\\\ -29\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186539864 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 22\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186539914 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}25 \\\\ -11\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186540028 :question/problem "Given the function \n$f(x;w)=2{w}_{1}{x}_{1}-5{w}_{2}{x}_{2},$\ncompute ${∇}_{w}\\,f$ with the input $$\n\\displaystyle x={\\begin{bmatrix}-2,\\,8\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}.\n$$\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" rowspan=\"2\" width=\"30px\">${∇}_{w}\\,f=\\text{ }$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [17592186540028 :question/worked-solution "To compute ${∇}_{w}\\,f$, we take partial derivatives with respect to each component of $w.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{w}_{1}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{w}_{1}}(2{w}_{1}{x}_{1}-5{w}_{2}{x}_{2})=2{x}_{1} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{w}_{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{w}_{2}}(2{w}_{1}{x}_{1}-5{w}_{2}{x}_{2})=-5{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, with $$\n\\displaystyle x={\\begin{bmatrix}-2,\\,8\\end{bmatrix}}^{T},\n$$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{w}\\,f & =\\begin{bmatrix}2{x}_{1} \\\\ -5{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -40\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186540034 :question/worked-solution "To compute ${∇}_{a,b}\\,f$, we take partial derivatives with respect to $a$ and $b.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂a} & =\\frac{∂}{∂a}(ax+by)=x \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂b} & =\\frac{∂}{∂b}(ax+by)=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{a,b}\\,f & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂a} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂b}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186540048 :question/problem "Consider the function $L:{R}^{2}\\to R$ defined by $L(x)=(Ax-2b)^{T}(Ax-2b)$ where \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{x}L(x)$ at $$\n\\displaystyle x=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$"] [17592186540048 :question/worked-solution "We can solve this problem by applying the chain rule for gradients.\n\nLet $y=Ax-2b.$ Then, $L(x)={y}^{T}y,$ and\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}L={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{T}={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{T}\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{T}.\n$$\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{T}y)=2{y}^{T}\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(Ax-2b)=A,\n$$\n\nwe have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}L & ={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{T}\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{T} \\\\  & ={A}^{T}\\cdot 2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $$\n\\displaystyle x=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}L & ={A}^{T}\\cdot 2y \\\\  & =2\\cdot {A}^{T}(Ax-2b) \\\\  & =2\\cdot {\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}(\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}-2\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}(\\begin{bmatrix}11 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}58 \\\\ 26\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186540056 :question/worked-solution "To compute ${∇}_{b,c}\\,f$, we take partial derivatives with respect to $b$ and $c.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂b} & =\\frac{∂}{∂b}(a{x}^{4}+b{x}^{3}+c{x}^{2})={x}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂c} & =\\frac{∂}{∂c}(a{x}^{4}+b{x}^{3}+c{x}^{2})={x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{b,c}\\,f & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂b} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂c}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{3} \\\\ {x}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186540057 :question/problem "Compute the gradient of the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R$ defined by\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}x\n$$\n\nwith respect to $x$ at  $$\n\\displaystyle x={\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}.\n$$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"30px\">$\\,{∇}_{x}f=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [17592186540057 :question/worked-solution "Defining the matrix $A$ as\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwe have $f(x)={x}^{T}Ax.$\n\nSince $A$ is not symmetric, we use the more general formula for the gradient of a quadratic function:\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}f(x)=({A}^{T}+A)x\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}+A & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, compute the gradient at $$\n\\displaystyle x=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}:\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}f(x) & =({A}^{T}+A)x \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0+2 \\\\ 0-4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186540065 :question/worked-solution "First, we compute ${∇}_{w}\\,f$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{w}_{1}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{w}_{1}}({w}_{1}{x}_{1}-{w}_{2}{x}_{2})^{2}=2{w}_{2}{x}_{2}({w}_{1}{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}) \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{w}_{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{w}_{2}}({w}_{1}{x}_{1}-{w}_{2}{x}_{2})^{2}=({w}_{1}{x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the second derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{w}_{1}^{2}}=2{w}_{2}{x}_{2}^{2}\\,\\, &  & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{w}_{1}∂{w}_{2}}=2{x}_{2}({w}_{1}{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}) \\\\  & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{w}_{2}∂{w}_{1}}=2{x}_{2}({w}_{1}{x}_{2}-{x}_{1})\\,\\, &  & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{w}_{2}^{2}}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{w}({∇}_{w}\\,f) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{w}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{w}_{1}∂{w}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{w}_{2}∂{w}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{w}_{2}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2{w}_{2}{x}_{2}^{2} & 2{x}_{2}({w}_{1}{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}) \\\\ 2{x}_{2}({w}_{1}{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}) & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186540066 :question/worked-solution "First, we take the variable subset gradient:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x,z}\\,f & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xyz) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂z}(xyz)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}yz \\\\ xy\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the second derivatives with respect to $x$ and $z$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\frac{∂}{∂x}(yz)=0\\,\\, &  & \\frac{∂}{∂z}(yz)=y \\\\  & \\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy)=y\\,\\, &  & \\frac{∂}{∂z}(xy)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x,z}({∇}_{x,z}\\,f) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂z} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂z∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{z}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & y \\\\ y & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186540068 :question/problem "Compute the gradient of the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R$ defined by\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 5 \\\\ -4 & -5\\end{bmatrix}x\n$$\n\nwith respect to $x$ at  $$\n\\displaystyle x={\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}.\n$$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"30px\">$\\,{∇}_{x}f=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [17592186540068 :question/worked-solution "Defining the matrix $A$ as\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 5 \\\\ -4 & -5\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwe have $f(x)={x}^{T}Ax.$\n\nSince $A$ is not symmetric, we use the more general formula for the gradient of a quadratic function:\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}f(x)=({A}^{T}+A)x\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -4 \\\\ 5 & -5\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}+A & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -4 \\\\ 5 & -5\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 5 \\\\ -4 & -5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -10\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, compute the gradient at $$\n\\displaystyle x=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}:\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}f(x) & =({A}^{T}+A)x \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -10\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0+1 \\\\ 0-10\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186540069 :question/problem "Compute the gradient of the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R$ defined by \n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}x\n$$\n\n with respect to $x$ at $$\n\\displaystyle x=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186540069 :question/worked-solution "Writing $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ we have\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{T}Ax.\n$$\n\nFor a symmetric matrix $A$, the gradient of the function $f(x)={x}^{T}Ax$ with respect to $x\\in {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}f(x)=2Ax.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $A$ is symmetric, we get\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}f(x)=2\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186540454 :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\times v & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 6 & 8 & 5 \\\\ -1 & -4 & -6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}8 & 5 \\\\ -4 & -6\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}6 & 5 \\\\ -1 & -6\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}6 & 8 \\\\ -1 & -4\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((8)(-6)-(5)(-4))i-((6)(-6)-(5)(-1))j+((6)(-4)-(8)(-1))k \\\\  & =(-48-(-20))i-(-36-(-5))j+(-24-(-8))k \\\\  & =-28i+31j-16k \\\\  & =⟨-28,31,-16⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186540456 :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\times v & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 3 & \\frac{7}{2} & -2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & -1 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{7}{2} & -2 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 5\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}3 & \\frac{7}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & -1\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right)(5)-(-2)(-1))i-((3)(5)-(-2)\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right))j+((3)(-1)-\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right)\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right))k \\\\  & =(\\frac{35}{2}-(2))i-(15-(-1))j+(-3-\\left(\\frac{7}{4}\\right))k \\\\  & =\\frac{31}{2}i-16j-\\frac{19}{4}k \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{31}{2},-16,-\\frac{19}{4}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186540471 :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\times v & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} & 2 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 1 & \\frac{7}{2}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & \\frac{7}{2}\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{3}{5} & 5 \\\\ 2 & \\frac{7}{2}\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{3}{5} & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((2)\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right)-(5)(1))i-(\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right)-(5)(2))j+(\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)(1)-(2)(2))k \\\\  & =(7-(5))i-(\\frac{21}{10}-(10))j+(\\frac{3}{5}-(4))k \\\\  & =2i+\\frac{79}{10}j-\\frac{17}{5}k \\\\  & =⟨2,\\frac{79}{10},-\\frac{17}{5}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186540476 :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\times v & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 8 & -1 & -4 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 4\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}8 & -4 \\\\ 4 & 4\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}8 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -4\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((-1)(4)-(-4)(-4))i-((8)(4)-(-4)(4))j+((8)(-4)-(-1)(4))k \\\\  & =(-4-(16))i-(32-(-16))j+(-32-(-4))k \\\\  & =-20i-48j-28k \\\\  & =⟨-20,-48,-28⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186540482 :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\times v & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 4 & 2 & -\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ -4 & 4 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ 4 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}4 & -\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ -4 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 4\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((2)\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)-\\left(-\\frac{4}{3}\\right)(4))i-((4)\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)-\\left(-\\frac{4}{3}\\right)(-4))j+((4)(4)-(2)(-4))k \\\\  & =(\\frac{2}{3}-\\left(-\\frac{16}{3}\\right))i-(\\frac{4}{3}-\\left(\\frac{16}{3}\\right))j+(16-(-8))k \\\\  & =6i+4j+24k \\\\  & =⟨6,4,24⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186540483 :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\times v & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -7 & 8 & 8 \\\\ 6 & -5 & -7\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}8 & 8 \\\\ -5 & -7\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-7 & 8 \\\\ 6 & -7\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-7 & 8 \\\\ 6 & -5\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((8)(-7)-(8)(-5))i-((-7)(-7)-(8)(6))j+((-7)(-5)-(8)(6))k \\\\  & =(-56-(-40))i-(49-(48))j+(35-(48))k \\\\  & =-16i-j-13k \\\\  & =⟨-16,-1,-13⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186540489 :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\times v & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ -\\frac{5}{2} & 1 & \\frac{5}{4}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & \\frac{5}{4}\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ -\\frac{5}{2} & \\frac{5}{4}\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ -\\frac{5}{2} & 1\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((-1)\\left(\\frac{5}{4}\\right)-(-2)(1))i-((-1)\\left(\\frac{5}{4}\\right)-(-2)\\left(-\\frac{5}{2}\\right))j+((-1)(1)-(-1)\\left(-\\frac{5}{2}\\right))k \\\\  & =(-\\frac{5}{4}-(-2))i-(-\\frac{5}{4}-(5))j+(-1-\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right))k \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}i+\\frac{25}{4}j-\\frac{7}{2}k \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{3}{4},\\frac{25}{4},-\\frac{7}{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186540496 :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\times v & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 4 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 6 & 1 & 7\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 7\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 6 & 7\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 1\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((3)(7)-(2)(1))i-((4)(7)-(2)(6))j+((4)(1)-(3)(6))k \\\\  & =(21-(2))i-(28-(12))j+(4-(18))k \\\\  & =19i-16j-14k \\\\  & =⟨19,-16,-14⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186540497 :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\times v & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 8\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -4\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((2)(8)-(1)(-4))i-((-3)(8)-(1)(4))j+((-3)(-4)-(2)(4))k \\\\  & =(16-(-4))i-(-24-(4))j+(12-(8))k \\\\  & =20i+28j+4k \\\\  & =⟨20,28,4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186540499 :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\times v & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -\\frac{6}{5} & 2 & 1 \\\\ -\\frac{7}{2} & -4 & 6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 6\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-\\frac{6}{5} & 1 \\\\ -\\frac{7}{2} & 6\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-\\frac{6}{5} & 2 \\\\ -\\frac{7}{2} & -4\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((2)(6)-(1)(-4))i-(\\left(-\\frac{6}{5}\\right)(6)-(1)\\left(-\\frac{7}{2}\\right))j+(\\left(-\\frac{6}{5}\\right)(-4)-(2)\\left(-\\frac{7}{2}\\right))k \\\\  & =(12-(-4))i-(-\\frac{36}{5}-\\left(-\\frac{7}{2}\\right))j+(\\frac{24}{5}-(-7))k \\\\  & =16i+\\frac{37}{10}j+\\frac{59}{5}k \\\\  & =⟨16,\\frac{37}{10},\\frac{59}{5}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186540530 :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186540553 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186540567 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{7} \\\\ -\\frac{6}{7} \\\\ \\frac{3}{7}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186540591 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{6}{7} \\\\ \\frac{2}{7} \\\\ \\frac{3}{7}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186540593 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186540631 :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186540646 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186540648 :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ 1 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186540661 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186540672 :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186540689 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2} \\\\ -\\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186540709 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186540719 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5}\\end{bmatrix}"]}
